From: PortareOS <hello@portare.org>
Subject: [PATCH] pcie: bind the sm8550 pcie0 phy to the root port

Move phys from the pcie0 controller node onto its root port and drop
phy-names, so the controller driver does not claim the PHY itself.

Only pcie0 is described here. pcie1 carried the same change plus a
pcie1_port0 label, and both went with the AYANEO device trees: nothing
this fork builds enables pcie1, and nothing refers to that label any
more.

On the Nova pcie0 is the WLAN link, so this is the half that matters -
and the half to reach for first if PCIe or wifi misbehaves after a
kernel bump, since dropping phy-names means the qcom-pcie driver looks
up a PHY it will never find.

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm8550.dtsi b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm8550.dtsi
index 912525e9bca6..a8eccaebcf2d 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm8550.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm8550.dtsi
@@ -2033,9 +2033,6 @@ &mc_virt SLAVE_EBI1 QCOM_ICC_TAG_ALWAYS>,
 
 			power-domains = <&gcc PCIE_0_GDSC>;
 
-			phys = <&pcie0_phy>;
-			phy-names = "pciephy";
-
 			operating-points-v2 = <&pcie0_opp_table>;
 
 			status = "disabled";
@@ -2100,6 +2097,8 @@ pcieport0: pcie@0 {
 				#address-cells = <3>;
 				#size-cells = <2>;
 				ranges;
+
+				phys = <&pcie0_phy>;
 			};
 		};
 
